(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-quiz"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is vascular imaging usually skipped for an irreversibly ischemic limb?","choices":["Pulses are still palpable","The limb recovers without care","Amputation is needed regardless","Contrast would worsen the limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vascular imaging is generally not immediately needed for an irreversibly damaged limb because nonviable extremities require major amputation regardless of the therapy instituted. Imaging guides salvage, and this limb, with profound sensory loss, paralysis and inaudible arterial and venous signals, cannot be salvaged. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How are most thoracic aortic aneurysms found?","choices":["By blue toe syndrome","Incidentally on chest radiograph","By sudden tearing chest pain","By a pulsatile abdominal mass"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most thoracic aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and found incidentally on chest radiograph, which may show widening of the mediastinal shadow or displacement of the trachea or left mainstem bronchus. A history of hypertension is common, and symptoms, when present, depend on location. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Where is ischemic rest pain usually localized?","choices":["Calf muscle","Buttock and hip","Front of the thigh","Forefoot and toes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain is constant, described as throbbing, burning or severe shooting pain, associated with coolness of the feet, and typically localized in the forefoot and toes, the most distal part of the extremity. It worsens with elevation or reclining and is poorly controlled even by narcotics. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What defines acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Sudden occlusion of a patent artery","Gradual narrowing over decades","Pain in calf muscles with walking","Swelling from blocked leg veins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute limb ischemia is the sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery, with abrupt onset of severe pain and absent pulses in the involved extremity, and it is a vascular emergency. Causes are local thrombosis at a stenotic artery or an embolus, most often a thromboembolus from the heart. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which procedure is the gold standard for high-grade carotid stenosis?","choices":["Carotid bypass grafting","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Antiplatelet therapy alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid endarterectomy removes plaque from inside the artery through a surgical incision and is the gold standard for high-grade carotid stenosis. Its complications are stroke or nerve damage, and carotid stenting is the less invasive alternative for poor surgical candidates. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which study is the choice for initial abdominal aortic aneurysm screening?","choices":["Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abdominal ultrasound is the diagnostic study of choice for initial screening for the presence of an aneurysm. It is also used for serial documentation of aneurysm size, with surveillance every three years at 3.0 to 3.9 cm and every six to 12 months at 4.0 to 5.4 cm. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What is a classic contraindication to magnetic resonance angiography?","choices":["Contrast allergy","Renal impairment","Diabetes","A pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance angiography is contraindicated with pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips. It serves as the alternative to computed tomography angiography in patients with a contrast allergy, and like the other angiographic studies it is used mainly to guide a planned intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which complication can a nonhealing ischemic toe ulcer lead to?","choices":["Osteomyelitis","Aortic dissection","Carotid stenosis","Pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic skin ulcers are painful, often begin as minor traumatic wounds, and fail to heal because blood supply cannot meet the increased demands of healing tissue. They sit at the most distal parts of the extremity, are often surrounded by dry gangrene, and can lead to osteomyelitis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What may the electrocardiogram show in aortic dissection?","choices":["Diagnostic Q waves","It may be normal","Always ST elevation","Always low voltage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so a normal tracing does not rule it out. The diagnosis rests on computed tomography of the chest and abdomen to delineate extent, magnetic resonance imaging, which is good but slower, or transesophageal echocardiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"Which condition is a relative contraindication to computed tomography angiography?","choices":["A prior bypass graft","Decreased renal function","A cardiac pacemaker","Intracranial aneurysm clips"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography uses contrast to delineate arterial anatomy, so decreased renal function and contrast allergy are its listed contraindications. Magnetic resonance angiography is the alternative in contrast allergy, though it carries its own contraindications of pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which history is present in most cases of aortic dissection?","choices":["Recent cardiac catheterization","Diabetes","Recent viral illness","Hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A history of hypertension is present in most cases of aortic dissection. The typical patient is in the 60s or 70s, outside Marfan syndrome, and male more often than female, and the most common predisposing factor is degeneration of medial collagen and elastin. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which test is the most widely used initial screening tool for carotid artery disease?","choices":["Plain neck radiography","Duplex ultrasonography","Digital subtraction angiography","Magnetic resonance angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Duplex ultrasonography is the most widely used initial screening tool for carotid artery disease. It is also commonly used to monitor patients serially for progression of disease or after intervention, while digital subtraction angiography remains the invasive gold standard used to guide therapy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Where do ischemic ulcers of critical limb ischemia usually appear?","choices":["Toe tips and between digits","Inner ankle above the heel","Front of the lower shin","Back of the calf"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic ulcers of critical limb ischemia form at the termination of arterial branches, the tips of the toes and between the digits, and at areas of increased focal pressure such as the metatarsal heads. They are dry, punched out and painful, with minimal bleeding. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When is surgery advised for asymptomatic carotid stenosis?","choices":["Over 80% and life expectancy over 5 years","Over 50% and life expectancy over 1 year","Over 70% and life expectancy over 2 years","Over 90% at any life expectancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surgical intervention is advised for asymptomatic patients with more than 80% stenosis and a life expectancy of more than five years. Symptomatic patients qualify at more than 70%, and patients with low-grade stenosis under 50% are managed medically. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which imaging is most widely used for thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Duplex ultrasonography","Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the most widely used study for thoracic aortic aneurysm and visualizes the entire thoracic and abdominal aorta. Magnetic resonance angiography gives location, extent and size but is time consuming, and transesophageal echocardiography evaluates the aortic valve and aortic root. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 74, 94, 106"},{"q":"Which antiplatelet agents are used in medical management of carotid disease?","choices":["Esmolol or nitroprusside","Pravastatin or simvastatin","Aspirin or clopidogrel","Heparin or alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin or clopidogrel prevents platelet aggregation in carotid artery disease. Medical management also treats comorbidities, with antihypertensives, improved glycemic control and statins, plus smoking cessation, healthy diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What defines aortic dissection?","choices":["A cavity outside the vessel wall","Dilation of all three layers","Plaque narrowing of the lumen","An intimal tear with a false lumen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection forms from an intimal tear and is typically contained by the media layer. The vessel wall layers separate, creating a false lumen within the media where blood can pool. Its most common predisposing factor is degeneration of medial collagen and elastin. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"Isolated groin pain from an expanding abdominal aortic aneurysm reflects pressure on which nerve?","choices":["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Phrenic nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm causes pain with expansion: abdominal, back or flank pain, pain radiating to the low back, and isolated groin pain from pressure on the femoral nerve. Early satiety and embolic blue toe syndrome can also occur. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which group has the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Caucasian men","Hispanic women","Asian men","African American women"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Caucasian men have the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm. Men outnumber women 2:1 under age 80, evening out to 1:1 over 80, and aneurysm is uncommon in African American, Asian and Hispanic populations. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which medical therapy is started in acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Oral pentoxifylline","Intravenous heparin","Oral cilostazol","Oral pravastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The medical therapy for acute peripheral arterial occlusion is anticoagulation with intravenous heparin, alongside an immediate vascular surgery consult. Diagnostic testing depends on the category of ischemia, and surgical options include catheter-directed thrombolysis, thromboembolectomy, bypass and amputation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"When palpating an ischemic leg, what does a line where the skin temperature changes indicate?","choices":["The rough level of the occlusion","How far an infection has spread","The point where venous reflux begins","The exact degree of narrowing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Skin temperature reflects the blood flow rate in dermal vessels and is used as a marker of perfusion, so the ischemic limb is cool. Palpated with the back of the hand, a demarcation of temperature gives a rough indication of the level of the occlusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 33, 42"},{"q":"A ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm is often misdiagnosed as which condition?","choices":["Carotid stenosis","Critical limb ischemia","Peripheral neuropathy","Renal colic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sudden escape of blood into the retroperitoneal space causes severe abdominal or back pain, a pulsatile abdominal mass and hypotension, the classic presentation in 50% of patients. Rupture is often misdiagnosed as renal colic, diverticulitis, gastrointestinal hemorrhage or ischemic bowel. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Failure of which structural proteins underlies aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Keratin and collagen of the intima","Actin and myosin of the adventitia","Fibrin and platelets of the lumen","Elastin and collagen of the media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic aneurysm reflects failure of the major structural proteins, elastin and collagen, in the media layer of the aorta. The cause is unclear, but genetic predisposition exists in Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and atherosclerosis may cause mechanical weakening. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which plaque is the vulnerable, unstable type?","choices":["Calcified core, thick media layer","Fatty core, thin fibrous shell","Small core, thick fibrous shell","Fatty streak, no fibrous shell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A vulnerable, unstable plaque develops with a fatty core and a thin fibrous outer shell. It can rupture into the bloodstream, and thrombosis, the clotting of blood, begins at the rupture site; if the clot enlarges to block the artery, the tissues it supplies begin to die below the blockage. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 6 (slide 6 pictures)"},{"q":"Which syndrome results when atheroemboli occlude the digital arteries?","choices":["Carotid steal syndrome","Compartment syndrome","Blue toe syndrome","Leriche syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris lodge in small vessels, and digital vascular occlusion causes ischemia and blue toe syndrome. Localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis occur at the sites of emboli, digital necrosis and gangrene may develop, and distal pulses usually remain palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which cause of leg pain is often present at rest and worsened by exercise?","choices":["Hip or knee osteoarthritis","Intermittent claudication","Venous insufficiency","Peripheral neuropathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osteoarthritis of the hip or knee is often present at rest and exacerbated by exercise, and the pain may improve when weight is taken off the joint. Claudication, by contrast, is brought on by activity and relieved by rest, so pain already present at rest points to the joint. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Within which layer of the aortic wall does the false lumen of an aortic dissection form?","choices":["The tunica intima","The tunica adventitia","The tunica media","Outside the wall entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A dissection forms from an intimal tear and is typically contained by the media layer, the muscular middle coat: the vessel wall layers separate, and a false lumen forms within the media, where blood can pool. Degeneration of medial collagen and elastin is the most common predisposing factor. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 3, 112, 114"},{"q":"Catheter-directed thrombolysis is indicated for acute limb ischemia of what duration?","choices":["Under 24 hours","Under 6 months","Under 3 hours","Under 14 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis is indicated for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration, with alteplase as the thrombolytic drug. The other surgical options are thromboembolectomy by catheter or surgery, arterial bypass and amputation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which pseudoaneurysms may be managed by watching and waiting?","choices":["Those in the aorta","Those causing nerve pain","Those under 2 cm","Those over 5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm under 2 cm in diameter may undergo spontaneous thrombosis and regression, so watch and wait is appropriate. Otherwise, ultrasound-guided injection of thrombin, which promotes clot, seals the leak. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which feature of exertional leg pain points to spinal stenosis rather than arterial claudication?","choices":["Eased by slowing the pace","Eased by leaning forward","Eased by standing still","Worse when legs are raised"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spinal stenosis causes claudication-like symptoms with numbness and weakness, relieved by rest and by sitting down or leaning forward to straighten out the lumbar spine. Arterial claudication is relieved by rest alone, usually within about 10 minutes, so posture-dependent relief points to the spine. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 20, 21, 41"}]};
